Announced in 2021: $4.6 bn, 780,000 t/a of ammonia production for export at Nelson Mandela Bay, built by a consortium of Hive Energy, BuiltAfrica and Linde, with full capacity to be reached in 2026.

The chain:
1. a 1,350 MW solar farm with battery storage - the primary energy source; the battery smooths the daily profile so that the downstream chemistry sees a steadier load;
2. seawater desalination, off-site, because electrolysis needs about 9 kg of demineralised water per kg of hydrogen, roughly double that including cooling, and the site is on an arid coast;
3. electrolysis, for the hydrogen;
4. air separation, for the nitrogen, which the green route no longer gets for free from combustion air;
5. Haber Bosch, for the ammonia;
6. the port itself, for export.

This is exactly the three-step renewable route of slide 647, with the two utilities - power and water - made explicit. What the component list says about the economics: 1,350 MW of solar in South Africa yields on the order of 2,500 full load hours, so the electrolyser and the synthesis loop must be sized far below the solar nameplate, and the mismatch between an intermittent supply and a Haber-Bosch loop that wants above 75 % load is precisely the problem the battery and a hydrogen buffer have to solve. The desalination unit is the item most often forgotten in exam answers, and it is the reason arid export sites have a cost item that a European plant does not.
